StringBuilder's fmt fallback includes the wrong header.

    "fmt::v7::detail::iterator_buffer<char*, char, fixed_buffer_traits>::flush()",
        referenced from: PS::StringBuilder::Append<unsigned int>(unsigned int)

ps/strings/StringBuilder.h uses std::to_chars when libc++ advertises it and
fmt::format_to_n otherwise - and for that fallback it includes <fmt/core.h>.
core.h *declares* iterator_buffer::flush(); the definition is in format.h. So
the fallback cannot link, and nobody notices, because libc++ defines
__cpp_lib_to_chars nearly everywhere.

Here it does not: with a deployment target of iOS 13 libc++ withholds
__cpp_lib_to_chars (the floating point part of to_chars is only available from
iOS 16.3), the fallback is taken, and the link fails on that one symbol. The fix
is the header that has the definition. Raising the deployment target would also
make it go away, by never taking the branch - which is how it stayed unnoticed.

--- source/ps/strings/StringBuilder.h
+++ source/ps/strings/StringBuilder.h
@@ -29,7 +29,12 @@
 #if defined(__cpp_lib_to_chars)
 #include <charconv>
 #else
-#include <fmt/core.h>
+// format.h, not core.h: fmt::format_to_n needs iterator_buffer::flush(), which
+// core.h only declares -- the definition is in format.h. This branch is rarely
+// taken, because libc++ defines __cpp_lib_to_chars almost everywhere, so the
+// missing definition never shows up as anything but an undefined symbol at the
+// very end of a link.
+#include <fmt/format.h>
 #endif
 
 namespace PS
